WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
Film Courage: Without naming names,&nbsp;
can you tell us a story about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:06.800
a studio took was it your&nbsp;
script or one of your ideas?

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.520
Shane Stanley, Filmmaker/Author/Instructor: Which&nbsp;
time? I’m a really big proponent on protecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.960
your work. I will go on record to say don’t waste&nbsp;
your time or your $20.00 or $30.00 doing WGA West.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:21.360
It’s a waste of time, it doesn’t do&nbsp;
anything. It’s not going to do what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:27.040
you want it to do. Spend the extra $25.00 and&nbsp;
go with the USCO (U.S. Library of Congress)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.440
US Copyright office is what that stands&nbsp;
for. The process is just as easy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:41.760
The copyrights last forever not five years.&nbsp;
Copyrights cover your legal expenses to defend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:48.880
the fact that you got ripped off and as we’re&nbsp;
young and naive coming up in Hollywood we tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:54.400
really love to share our work with people and I&nbsp;
always tell people don’t you dare send a script&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:59.600
to anybody until it’s finished and copy written&nbsp;
because it’s too easy the person you are sending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:04.480
it to could steal it. The person that you sent it&nbsp;
to could send it to a friend who needs an idea or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:07.520
hey I’m thinking about getting into screenwriting.&nbsp;
Do you have any scripts laying around that I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.040
look at and kind of use as a model? The easiest&nbsp;
thing for a new writer is to look at somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:16.720
else’s work and just copy it. It’s what happens.&nbsp;
I’ve been ripped of over the years. I actually had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:26.160
a script get produced by a studio who I’ll&nbsp;
not name. They actually went out and made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:32.000
the movie. I was a 9 million dollar low-budget&nbsp;
film that they put the money behind to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:36.720
and they were dumb enough not to even change&nbsp;
the title of the film or the character names&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:41.200
so I guess whoever brought it to them took&nbsp;
my work and just put their name on the cover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:47.600
sheet and just did it as is. I had gotten wind of&nbsp;
this when they were in post production and what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.400
you’re supposed to do when you get ripped off&nbsp;
is you let the film come out like let it get on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:57.600
the books and let the trailers start hitting and&nbsp;
that Monday before the Friday is when you drop it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.400
because they are so vested in this with the&nbsp;
20 million in P&amp;A [Prints and Advertising] or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:07.680
whatever it is they’re going to work out a deal&nbsp;
with you and instead stupid here knew somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.840
who knew somebody who worked at the studio when&nbsp;
I heard it was in post-production I made noise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:15.760
about it and then the film just disappeared. It&nbsp;
never came out, it was never finished but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.920
indeed my friend got me a copy of the script.&nbsp;
There was a polish on the script. They kept the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:26.160
names the same, kept the title the same. I go on&nbsp;
IMDB, it’s still there “unreleased” 15 years later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:32.560
but it’s just kind of funny that this movie got&nbsp;
made that never even got finished (no release).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:41.200
So that was one. I’ve had agencies take projects&nbsp;
and give them to show runners days after we meet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:50.000
on them, sometimes hours after. I’ve had a project&nbsp;
that was packaged by a major agency, then in 2007&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.560
when the crash hit my investors backed out.&nbsp;
We were three weeks away from rolling film and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:01.120
the ’07 recession hit. My investors had to&nbsp;
back out so the project never got made and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:07.920
weirdly enough two years later the packaging&nbsp;
agent got a new client who shot pretty much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.520
the same script, wound up making it&nbsp;
into Sundance and doing quite well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:16.480
and at least they changed the title.&nbsp;
They changed the character names&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:20.080
and the best part was they changed the opening&nbsp;
scene but the rest of movie was pretty much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:27.760
in step. I don’t make noise about that stuff&nbsp;
anymore and I’ve learned. That’s probably why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.240
I’ve become an independent filmmaker. If I have an&nbsp;
idea that I’m passionate about, I’m not sending it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:37.200
to you, I’m just going to go make it. That’s where&nbsp;
I’ve gotten. I’m just not going to share my work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:42.160
with anybody. If there is something I believe in&nbsp;
and want to do, it stays in-house and we’ll do it.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:45.520
Film Courage: So that’s how&nbsp;
a lot of people do it if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:48.400
feel their ideas [scripts] have been&nbsp;
stolen? They wait until the week of?

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:51.840
Shane: Well you will often hear about&nbsp;
a film that comes out and you will hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.160
writes are making noises that their idea was&nbsp;
taken and that is usually when the film is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:01.120
coming out and what happens is that you get the&nbsp;
studio or the network so vested in the project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:05.120
they’ve completely paid the entire process,&nbsp;
now they are going through the marketing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:08.640
P&amp;A [Prints and Advertising] and they’ve committed&nbsp;
a good year (year and a half) to the project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:14.480
and then all of the sudden it’s coming out. And&nbsp;
you know it’s not common but it’s not rare where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:19.520
you hear this movie is coming out and there are a&nbsp;
couple of writers who say they were ripped off and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:24.240
more often than not those stories disappear and&nbsp;
go away and I think if you’re able to prove it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:31.280
I always say you have to take copious notes,&nbsp;
I always say have a back-up email address so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:35.600
anytime you send a script to somebody, you send&nbsp;
it to another address you have. Keep those things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:39.760
print them out, save them on a cloud so you&nbsp;
have a track record. If you send a script&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:42.000
to somebody you have a conversation&nbsp;
with somebody, keep a journey “Hey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.120
I spoke to this agent at this agency on this&nbsp;
date about this actor and this project.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:55.520
Because you know if you’re ever going to make&nbsp;
noise that you were ripped off you have to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:01.840
chapter and verse, copious notes on everything&nbsp;
that you’ve done which ideas don’t cut it anymore.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:06.720
You have to show that there was an interaction.&nbsp;
Well it’s kind of like the guys who pointed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:11.520
finger at Zuck for Facebook, I mean they probably&nbsp;
got two, three, four hundred million dollars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:16.000
It was an “undisclosed settlement” but they were&nbsp;
able to prove this guy took their platform and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.960
did something else with it and that you have to&nbsp;
think of the same way. You have to have blueprints&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:26.000
for your work, you have to have detailed journal.&nbsp;
You have to have your communications and when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:32.160
were, what they were, who they were with so yes&nbsp;
and copyrighted with the Library of Congress.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:39.280
Film Courage: So is that true that it’s easier for&nbsp;
Hollywood to just deal with the lawsuit than pay?

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:42.480
Shane: I think everybody likes dealing&nbsp;
with a lawsuit than going through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:46.960
painstaking…because everybody&nbsp;
today wants the easy way out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:52.240
It’s easy to rip off things, it’s too easy&nbsp;
to take advantage. I know a very prominent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:55.600
producer who has since passed away so I’m going&nbsp;
to tell the story without mentioning a name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:02.160
you know he found a script, he was a very&nbsp;
successful producer and he had been nominated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:09.040
for an Oscar and found a script from an unknown&nbsp;
writer who wasn’t in the WGA or anything and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:16.320
basically worked out a deal to pay the guy peanuts&nbsp;
and it wound up becoming a very successful trilogy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:22.160
and you know just bought and sold the script over&nbsp;
and over until he got it where it needed to go and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:28.480
you know it just seems like Hollywood is kind&nbsp;
of copying itself anyway. I mean that’s what it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:33.680
does right? Every story has been told, it’s how we&nbsp;
tell it. You know shark movies are big right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:38.000
let’s make a shark movie. Football movies are&nbsp;
big right now, let’s do a football movie. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.560
capitalized on that Gridiron Gang. We had We Are&nbsp;
Marshall, we had Invincible, we had We Are Giants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:48.640
we had Facing The Giants. Yeah Friday Night&nbsp;
Lights. You had this movement of football movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:52.720
and it’s no different. I mean that’s what&nbsp;
they do. Incidentally is the same guy who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:59.040
took that script for peanuts it turned out&nbsp;
he admitted to me later he was trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:03.200
make Gridiron Gang with another studio and he&nbsp;
never had the rights to do it. So it goes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:10.720
show you people will easily take an idea and run&nbsp;
with it and he never had the rights to do it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.880
You know how do you feel when somebody rips off&nbsp;
your work. I guess the old saying is ‘Imitation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:23.360
is the greatest form of flattery.’ But when&nbsp;
you’re reading the front page of trades and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:28.960
a big star is attached to a project that you&nbsp;
walked into an agency or a network you know my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:35.360
dad always talks about how he had brought in&nbsp;
a script to a studio back in the early ’70’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:45.680
and was told that’s not for us and&nbsp;
then years later [hums theme music].&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:52.320
So I learned be careful with your product but&nbsp;
how does it feel when you’re ripped off, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:59.440
hurts because you have that, it’s almost if you’ve&nbsp;
ever walked into your house after it’s been robbed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:09.680
it’s the ultimate betrayal, it’s the ultimate form&nbsp;
of…it’s a violation. I don’t know why somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:14.480
can’t say ‘Hey, kid you wrote a script, it’s&nbsp;
pretty good. The idea is there and we wan’t to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:18.720
take it and work where we are going to pay you.’&nbsp;
I mean just the deception that happens with these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:23.440
things and I can’t emphasize enough that we have&nbsp;
to. I mean it feels empty. You look at that time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:30.160
and most writers especially write things on spec&nbsp;
because one day wouldn’t it be great and then one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:34.240
day you have a library of scripts that you can&nbsp;
look back and say these ten scripts but each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:37.600
script takes time whether you are a fast writer&nbsp;
or a slow writer. I know people who spend three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:41.360
months on a screenplay. I know people who spend&nbsp;
10 years on a screenplay. I know somebody who is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:49.840
actually on their 30th year on the same script.&nbsp;
I cannot imagine if that got ripped off. I mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:57.360
30 years, think about it.I tend to take things&nbsp;
well. I tend to take bad news well so I didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:01.680
get all wrapped up in it. I said okay lesson&nbsp;
learned. Chalk it up as a life lesson and move on.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:04.520
Film Courage: Did you get any&nbsp;
compensation for it Shane?

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:09.040
Shane: I never got compensated. There are three&nbsp;
projects that I did (that I created) that got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:16.000
taken. One was produced into a film that never&nbsp;
came out. The other one went into pretty hardcore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:19.360
development at a major network with a big&nbsp;
star attached. It never it never did come.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:29.360
There was another one that it was basically we had&nbsp;
a pretty good timeline and proof that there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:36.400
a rip-off and what was interesting was that the&nbsp;
institution (the studio whatever you want to call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:41.200
it) that did the ripping off had also optioned&nbsp;
another project from us previously. So what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:45.280
basically did is they took intellectual&nbsp;
property that they had optioned from us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.160
and put it into another project (a storyline&nbsp;
into another project) that they already greenlit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:54.080
Totally separate. They were going to make&nbsp;
that anyway. They were hoping to make ours.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:58.160
Our’s didn’t really come to fruition so they&nbsp;
kind of grabbed some stuff and threw it in there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:04.640
We hired the best of the best lawyers and they&nbsp;
were unbelievable in how they structured this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:08.400
case and their timelines. At the end of the day&nbsp;
nobody wants to go to court. Nobody wants to sue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:12.240
anybody. I’m a firm believe in the blacklist. I&nbsp;
know people (and I don’t mean the script side),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:16.320
I’m talking about people saying we’re not going to&nbsp;
work with him because…and it just got to a point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:24.640
where you know other deals, other options were&nbsp;
made so…do you get paid for it no. Sometimes if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:30.720
it’s handled in a certain way and I think right&nbsp;
you could possibly entertain another deal or two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:37.920
with an institution that would admit that maybe&nbsp;
they wronged you or were upset you felt wronged.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:44.640
You know optioning another piece of property&nbsp;
for two or three years isn’t uncommon I guess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:48.960
if you play your cards right. Basically what&nbsp;
happened is they said look you know alright we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:52.160
did what we did and if you guys want to go through&nbsp;
with it we’re going to bankrupt you in lawsuits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:55.760
it’s going to take 12 years to engage, do you&nbsp;
have something else we can option? And you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.760 --> 00:11:00.800
they are not going to make it but that’s how they&nbsp;
do it. That’s how it worked on the third (on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:06.400
first studio one). They will come up with creative&nbsp;
ways to make you feel or Hey why don’t we give you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:09.200
a development deal and then we don’t have to pay&nbsp;
you anything. They just take a few gratuitous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:12.240
meetings with you over the course of two years&nbsp;
and you feel like you got yours you know.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:19.040
It’s weird. It’s a deceptive business and I think&nbsp;
that is what has driven me. I don’t ever want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:24.160
thumb my nose at Hollywood proper. I’ve had a lot&nbsp;
of success in it and thank God for it and hope to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:30.960
work again in it. I don’t count on it and I don’t&nbsp;
care if I don’t. I went real rogue (independent)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:36.560
in what I do because I just got tired of dealing&nbsp;
with a machine. I got tired of developing. Fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:40.400
in love with an idea, put it together whether&nbsp;
you’ve got eight dollars or eight hundred thousand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:47.200
dollars and go do it. What’s stopping you? I mean&nbsp;
the next film I am doing we are starting in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:53.840
couple of months and it started because a group of&nbsp;
us were sick and tired of sitting around in 2020&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.400 --> 00:12:00.480
and we figured we can toss 2020 in the garbage bin&nbsp;
like a lot of people are or we can show something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:03.840
we have something to show for it. And it was&nbsp;
kind of like we know two guys with two great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:07.360
cameras and a sound guy. We’ve got actors that&nbsp;
want to play. I’ve got a great writer who has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:11.360
got a ton of material. I’ll direct, produce&nbsp;
and cut the thing for free. I don’t care. Let’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:16.160
make this year something. And then it turned into&nbsp;
something where investors have gotten involved and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:20.560
better actors and bigger actors and crew and now&nbsp;
all of the sudden we have a real movie we’re going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:24.640
to make and it happened because we just got sick&nbsp;
and tired of sitting around and started putting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:30.160
things into motion. We never asked anybody for&nbsp;
money. That was the last thing. We just wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:35.653
to get busy and have something to show for&nbsp;
2020 and the next thing you know we’re in full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.653 --> 00:12:51.840
blown pre-production on a show so I like that.&nbsp;
There is no development, there’s “let’s do it!”

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:53.954
Question For The Viewers:

00:12:53.954 --> 00:12:59.280
Which part of this video is most&nbsp;
helpful? How will this impact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.280 --> 00:12:59.943
you moving forward?

